[38;5;231m⏺[39m That collapses the project considerably — and it corrects something I said a message ago. I recommended an append-only
  change log to feed notifications. In a single-user app that's wrong: you're the only one making changes, and telling
  you about your own edits is noise. The triggers that matter here are time-based, which need due dates on tasks, not an
  event log. Dropping it.

  Revised decomposition, now two pieces:

  1. [1mTasks core[0m — model + CRUD + localStorage + rendering into that empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m
  2. [1mNotifications[0m — time-based reminders over those tasks

  One constraint to get on the table before we choose triggers, because it's the kind of thing that disappoints people
  after the fact: [1ma client-side page only runs while its tab is open.[0m There's no server and no cron. "Notify me at 9am
  that three things are overdue" cannot fire if the browser is closed — at best it fires next time you open the app.
  Genuine background delivery needs a service worker plus push infrastructure, which drags a server back in.

  So the honest version of client-side notifications is: [3mwhat you see when you're looking[0m, plus optional OS-level toasts
  while the tab is alive.

  With that said — what should actually fire one?
